---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import CaseStudyHeader from '../../components/notebook/CaseStudyHeader.astro';
import CaseHero from '../../components/notebook/CaseHero.astro';
import SectionRow from '../../components/notebook/SectionRow.astro';
import InsightLine from '../../components/notebook/InsightLine.astro';
import Fork from '../../components/notebook/Fork.astro';
import Figure from '../../components/notebook/Figure.astro';
import BeforeAfter from '../../components/notebook/BeforeAfter.astro';
import OutcomeStats from '../../components/notebook/OutcomeStats.astro';
import Reflection from '../../components/notebook/Reflection.astro';
import ProgressRail from '../../components/notebook/ProgressRail.astro';
import { nextCaseStudy } from '../../data/entries';

const next = nextCaseStudy('biomotivate');

import cover from '../../assets/biomotivate/cover.png';
import flyer1 from '../../assets/biomotivate/flyer-1.png';
import flyer2 from '../../assets/biomotivate/flyer-2.png';
import beforeDiscover from '../../assets/biomotivate/before-discover.png';
import afterDiscover from '../../assets/biomotivate/after-discover.png';

/*
 * Copy rules for this page (see notes/intake-biomotivate.md):
 * - Every fact comes from the intake, the flyers, or the screenshots. Nothing invented.
 * - [TODO: …] marks what only Varrshitha can fill in. Leave them visible.
 * - Patients are in addiction recovery: no names, no identifying details,
 *   no generated images of patients, quotes paraphrased.
 */
---

<Base title="Biomotivate · VK">
  <div style="background: var(--paper); min-height: 100vh; color: var(--ink); padding-bottom: 120px">

    <CaseStudyHeader entryNum="03" />
    <ProgressRail />

    <CaseHero
      entryNum="03"
      eyebrow="Biomotivate"
      title="Biomotivate"
      lede="I was hired to fix the interface of a VR therapy platform at an addiction recovery house. The real problem was that patients had stopped finding anything in it worth watching."
      meta={[
        { label: "Role", value: "Product intern" },
        { label: "Timeline", value: "Summer 2025, 4 months" },
        { label: "Team", value: "Founder, 1 engineer, psychology and data science interns" },
        { label: "Focus", value: "Product, UX research, AI video" },
        { label: "Results", value: "+60% engagement, +40% new patients" },
      ]}
      cover={afterDiscover}
      coverAlt="The redesigned Discover screen: navigation sidebar with Discover, Emotional Resilience Sessions, Internet Positive Videos, and My Saved Videos."
      heroSlotPlaceholder="After-redesign hero screenshot — full app screen at 1x, exported from FlutterFlow. Replace cover.png with the final hi-fi once available."
      note="handed: fix the UI.<br><br>turned out: why were patients who'd used it for a year walking away?<br><br><span style='color:#66625A'>(names and details are left out. these are people in recovery.)</span>"
    />

    <!-- 01 -->
    <SectionRow num="01" label="overview">
      <Fragment slot="main">
        <p class="body">
          Peace is a VR therapy platform made by Biomotivate, a Pittsburgh startup. At an addiction
          recovery house, patients used it as part of their routine: put on an Oculus headset and watch
          a short video that pairs a hard memory with something uplifting. Some of them had been doing
          this for over a year.
        </p>
        <p class="body">
          My job was the interface, but the team was also tracking a number that wasn't on my ticket:
          attendance had been falling since before I got there.
        </p>
      </Fragment>
      <Fragment slot="margin">
        <p class="note" style="margin:0;transform:rotate(-1deg)">
          I could have shipped the fixes and had a clean internship. I wanted to know why people were leaving.
        </p>
      </Fragment>
    </SectionRow>

    <!-- 02 -->
    <SectionRow num="02" label="research">
      <Fragment slot="main">
        <p class="body">
          So I went where the patients were. I ran VR sessions myself and sat through about 20
          think-alouds, watching people use Peace while they talked me through it.
        </p>
        <p class="body">
          Nobody struggled with the headset or the app: the long-time patients moved through it without
          a second thought. What they did instead was search, scrolling for a video they hadn't seen yet.
          Each session offered one required video and two optional ones, and almost nobody touched
          the optional ones.
        </p>
        <p class="body">
          If the videos helped, why skip them? If the app was easy, why stop coming? Were they bored
          with therapy itself?
        </p>
      </Fragment>
    </SectionRow>

    <!-- 03 -->
    <InsightLine
      num="03"
      label="insight"
      observed="Patients searched for videos they hadn't seen"
      assumed="That this, not the UI, was why attendance fell"
    >
      They weren't lost in the app. They were tired of what was in it.
    </InsightLine>

    <!-- 04 -->
    <Fork
      num="04"
      label="decision"
      context="That was my hunch. Before betting on it, I lined up every explanation I could think of for the drop."
      options={[
        { label: "The headset. VR takes getting used to.", pathNotTaken: "fair for new patients. it doesn't explain people who'd used it for a year." },
        { label: "The interface.", pathNotTaken: "the people leaving were the ones who knew the app best." },
        { label: "Understanding. Patients didn't know how the therapy was helping them.", pathNotTaken: "I tested this one first, with flyers. [TODO: what happened]" },
        { label: "The content. Nothing new, and nothing that felt like theirs.", chosen: true },
      ]}
      rationale="The headset and the interface couldn't explain why the people who knew them best were leaving. The flyers could explain the method, but understanding why something works isn't the same as wanting to do it. [TODO: confirm once the flyer outcome is in.] The content fit everything: the same library, watched for months, made for no one in particular."
      knew={[
        { text: "Long-time users were leaving, not just new ones", confidence: "observed" },
        { text: "Patients searched for videos they hadn't seen", confidence: "observed" },
        { text: "The optional videos were almost never picked", confidence: "observed" },
        { text: "That understanding the therapy would make people want to come", confidence: "assumed" },
      ]}
    />

    <Figure
      fig="fig. 1"
      caption="The flyers I designed for the house, explaining in plain language how Peace works and why it helps. This was my test of suspect C."
      note="Page two has a section called “What if I get bored?” Our answer was: there are 250+ videos."
      images={[
        { src: flyer1, alt: "Flyer, page one: what Peace is, how pairing hard memories with positive videos works, and long-term benefits." },
        { src: flyer2, alt: "Flyer, page two: why try it, paid opportunities, helping others, and what to do if you get bored." },
      ]}
    />

    <!-- 05 -->
    <Fork
      num="05"
      label="strategy"
      context="The team's first idea was more videos on more topics. But by our own flyer, the library already had 250+ videos, and people were still scrolling past them."
      options={[
        { label: "More stock videos, on more topics.", pathNotTaken: "if the library were small. ours wasn't." },
        { label: "Videos made from each patient's own memories and interests.", chosen: true },
      ]}
      rationale="The first personalized videos got rewatched. People asked to watch them in their free time and came back with ideas for more. The founder pushed back hard: personal videos are slow to make, they don't scale, you can't show one to anyone else, and they raise the privacy stakes. I brought the usability testing results and made the case for long-term value. [TODO: one line, in my voice, on how that conversation went]"
      knew={[
        { text: "The first personalized videos were rewatched and asked for again", confidence: "observed" },
        { text: "Some patients felt guilty skipping a video made for them", confidence: "observed" },
        { text: "That more topics wouldn't get anyone to rewatch", confidence: "assumed" },
      ]}
    />

    <section class="cs-row" style="padding-bottom: 104px">
      <div></div>
      <figure style="margin: 0">
        <blockquote class="quote">“I felt bad for not watching it, because you made it for me.”</blockquote>
        <figcaption class="mono" style="margin-top: 20px">A patient, paraphrased</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <aside class="cs-margin sw-note" style="padding-top: 10px">
        <p class="note" style="margin:0;transform:rotate(0.8deg)">
          This is where the hunch came from. It's also a bias: people might watch because they feel they owe us.
          Both are true, so it comes back in the results.
        </p>
      </aside>
    </section>

    <!-- 06 -->
    <SectionRow num="06" label="design">
      <Fragment slot="main">
        <p class="body">
          Personal videos need personal input, and asking people in recovery about their memories is
          delicate. An open-ended form (what interests you? what do you want to see?) felt intimidating.
        </p>
        <p class="body">
          So the form became a short set of guided questions for people who aren't sure what to say. It asks
          for a good memory, or an interest they'd like to see. I wrote it with the psychology postdoc on our
          team, who reviewed every question so none of them would lead someone back to their addiction or
          trauma.
        </p>
      </Fragment>
      <Fragment slot="margin">
        <p class="note" style="margin:0;transform:rotate(-0.6deg)">[TODO: one question that made it in, and one we cut]</p>
      </Fragment>
    </SectionRow>

    <!-- 07 -->
    <SectionRow num="07" label="execution">
      <Fragment slot="main">
        <p class="body">
          Personal also meant new ways to get it wrong. Characters changed from one clip to the next.
          Some ChatGPT-generated clips sexualized female characters, and one got a patient's body type wrong.
          When the video is about your own memory, that isn't a small bug.
        </p>
        <p class="body" style="margin-bottom: 14px">So I set a standard for every video:</p>
        <ol class="list">
          <li>One tool and one fixed prompt template, picked after benchmarking the options.</li>
          <li>Prompts written to keep characters consistent from clip to clip.</li>
          <li>A ChatGPT Project with our guidelines and an explicit list of what not to generate.</li>
          <li>A person reviews every generated image before a patient sees it.</li>
        </ol>
        <p class="body">
          Veo and Sora were out: too expensive, too slow, and not close enough to the story.
        </p>
      </Fragment>
      <Fragment slot="margin">
        <p class="note" style="margin:0;transform:rotate(-1deg)">
          AI made personal videos possible. Someone still had to make sure they were kind.
        </p>
      </Fragment>
    </SectionRow>

    <!-- 08 -->
    <SectionRow num="08" label="delivery" pb="48px">
      <Fragment slot="main">
        <p class="body">
          And the UI fixes I was hired for? I still did them, as part of a rebuild. Our only engineer worked
          offsite in JavaScript, and the personalized flow was a lot of new work. I proposed moving Peace to
          FlutterFlow, a free drag-and-drop builder, so I could build the fixes and the new flow myself.
        </p>
      </Fragment>
      <Fragment slot="margin">
        <p class="note" style="margin:0;transform:rotate(0.6deg)">[TODO: what we gave up by moving to FlutterFlow]</p>
      </Fragment>
    </SectionRow>

    <BeforeAfter
      fig="fig. 2"
      wide
      before={beforeDiscover}
      beforeAlt="Before: a single long form with a study-participant question, a video picker, a full-screen button and a 1–100 rating field."
      after={afterDiscover}
      afterAlt="After: the same form inside a navigation sidebar with Discover, Emotional Resilience Sessions, Internet Positive Videos and My Saved Videos."
      caption="The Discover screen, before and after. Drag the handle — or use arrow keys — to compare. Redesigned in Figma, built in FlutterFlow."
      note="the navigation sidebar was the whole fix. one decision, four destinations."
    />

    <!-- 09 -->
    <SectionRow num="09" label="outcomes" pb="104px">
      <Fragment slot="main">
        <OutcomeStats stats={[
          { number: "+60%", label: "engagement: more sessions and more repeat views" },
          { number: "+40%", label: "patients signing up for the first time" },
        ]} />
        <p class="mono" style="margin: 24px 0 40px">Baseline and time window: [TODO]</p>
        <p class="body">
          The full feature was never going to be finished in my three months, and I knew that going in.
          I left the team with a working flow, a standard for making the videos, and early evidence that
          personal works better than more.
        </p>
      </Fragment>
      <Fragment slot="margin">
        <p class="note" style="margin:0;transform:rotate(1deg)">
          Not a blind study. The same “we made it for you” feeling that gave me the hunch could be inflating
          these numbers. I'd rather say that than have you wonder.
        </p>
      </Fragment>
    </SectionRow>

    <!-- 10 -->
    <Reflection num="10" label="reflection">
      Pick the problem that matters over the deliverable that's safe, even if you won't be there to see it finished.
    </Reflection>

    <footer class="cs-row" style="align-items: end; padding-top: 24px">
      <div></div>
      <a href={`/${next.section}/${next.slug}`} class="next">
        <div class="mono" style="margin-bottom: 10px">Next entry &rarr;</div>
        <div class="next-q cs-next-q-wrap">{next.footerQ}</div>
      </a>
      <div></div>
    </footer>

  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .body {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: 21px;
    line-height: 1.62;
    margin: 0 0 22px;
    font-variation-settings: 'opsz' 20, 'wght' 400;
    max-width: none;
  }
  .body:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }

  .list {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: 21px;
    line-height: 1.55;
    margin: 0 0 22px;
    padding-left: 1.4em;
    font-variation-settings: 'opsz' 20, 'wght' 400;
  }
  .list li { margin-bottom: 8px; padding-left: 4px; }
  .list li::marker { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 13px; color: var(--ink-60); }

  .quote {
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    border: none;
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-style: italic;
    font-size: 36px;
    line-height: 1.25;
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
    font-variation-settings: 'opsz' 36, 'wght' 300;
    max-width: none;
  }

  .next { text-decoration: none; color: var(--ink); display: block; }
  .next-q {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: 30px;
    line-height: 1.2;
    text-wrap: balance;
  }
  .next:hover .next-q { text-decoration: underline; text-decoration-color: var(--pencil); text-underline-offset: 6px; }

  @media (max-width: 1000px) {
    .body, .list { font-size: 19px; }
    .quote { font-size: 28px; }
  }
</style>
